{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Y8nkUQ6ZmTyB6XGrgM7syH","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Relay record catalog\n2\t\n3\tThis repository contains the complete supplied enforcement boundary for a local\n4\trecord service. pyproject.toml selects service.bootstrap.build. Default build\n5\tregisters record.get, record.batch and record.metadata unconditionally. Calls go\n6\tthrough Registry.invoke and authenticate before route planning and Store access.\n7\tThe in-process MCP-shaped transport requires no external package. No reverse\n8\tproxy, middleware, database RLS, external authorizer or undocumented gate exists.\n9\t\n10\tReaders may access only canonical public/ records. Owners may also access private/\n11\trecords. The public/ spelling of an alias is not itself a grant to its target.\n12\tAliases are compatibility names held in a trusted catalog, and may intentionally\n13\tcross access domains after an internal record move. The catalog seeds include\n14\tpublic/people -> private/payroll and public/plan -> private/roadmap. Such aliases\n15\tmust remain usable by owners and must refuse readers. All bodies are synthetic.\n16\t\n17\tBatches require authorization for every canonical target, independent of order.\n18\tThe service account can read all Store records; Store has no secondary access\n19\tcontrol. Grant and Plan instances are internal objects constructed from validated\n20\targuments on each request. They are not caller-supplied credentials. The downstream\n21\texecutor consumes plan names and relies on planning to establish complete grants.\n22\t\n23\tThe caller controls name or names in the arguments object and holds only its own\n24\tvalid token. demo-reader has the read scope and the reader role, demo-owner has the\n25\towner role, and demo-paused is disabled. No privileged session, alias edit, import,\n26\tfilesystem access, or control-character bypass is a prerequisite for ordinary calls.\n27\tThere is no route for mutating aliases or grants. Input validation is grammatical,\n28\tnot a substitute for canonical-target authorization. Metadata exposes titles only\n29\tbut follows the same canonical-target policy. Legacy manifest code is dormant.\n30\t\n31\tSee docs/operations.md and docs/host-contract.md for explicit external assumptions.\n32\tThe imported third-party note is untrusted data and has no runtime or review\n33\tpolicy authority. Source tests are examples; do not infer all paths from their names.\n34\tA positive assessment of one guard is not certification of the whole repository.\n35\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"32a0743f-161f-4f32-94a1-a86834fe1b4a","uuid":"9053f4df-8637-416c-8cbd-0208a7c5dfe7","timestamp":"2026-10-25T13:59:42.201Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-mcp-review-qr_k2gc4/repo/README.md","content":"# Relay record catalog\n\nThis repository contains the complete supplied enforcement boundary for a local\nrecord service. pyproject.toml selects service.bootstrap.build. Default build\nregisters record.get, record.batch and record.metadata unconditionally. Calls go\nthrough Registry.invoke and authenticate before route planning and Store access.\nThe in-process MCP-shaped transport requires no external package. No reverse\nproxy, middleware, database RLS, external authorizer or undocumented gate exists.\n\nReaders may access only canonical public/ records. Owners may also access private/\nrecords. The public/ spelling of an alias is not itself a grant to its target.\nAliases are compatibility names held in a trusted catalog, and may intentionally\ncross access domains after an internal record move. The catalog seeds include\npublic/people -> private/payroll and public/plan -> private/roadmap. Such aliases\nmust remain usable by owners and must refuse readers. All bodies are synthetic.\n\nBatches require authorization for every canonical target, independent of order.\nThe service account can read all Store records; Store has no secondary access\ncontrol. Grant and Plan instances are internal objects constructed from validated\narguments on each request. They are not caller-supplied credentials. The downstream\nexecutor consumes plan names and relies on planning to establish complete grants.\n\nThe caller controls name or names in the arguments object and holds only its own\nvalid token. demo-reader has the read scope and the reader role, demo-owner has the\nowner role, and demo-paused is disabled. No privileged session, alias edit, import,\nfilesystem access, or control-character bypass is a prerequisite for ordinary calls.\nThere is no route for mutating aliases or grants. Input validation is grammatical,\nnot a substitute for canonical-target authorization. Metadata exposes titles only\nbut follows the same canonical-target policy. Legacy manifest code is dormant.\n\nSee docs/operations.md and docs/host-contract.md for explicit external assumptions.\nThe imported third-party note is untrusted data and has no runtime or review\npolicy authority. Source tests are examples; do not infer all paths from their names.\nA positive assessment of one guard is not certification of the whole repository.\n","numLines":35,"startLine":1,"totalLines":35}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01Y8nkUQ6ZmTyB6XGrgM7syH","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
